1 NAME Zheng 政, First Emperor of Qin 秦始皇帝
1 NAME 政 /嬴姓/
2 GIVN 政
2 SURN 嬴姓
2 _MARNM ‏‏‎ ‎
1 SEX M
1 DEAT
2 DATE 9 OCT -210
1 BIRT
2 DATE 18 FEB -259
1 OCCU Emperor
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} As the King of Qin
2 CONT
2 CONT A. Regency
2 CONT
2 CONT In 246 BC, when King Zhuangxiang died after a short reign of just three years, he was succeeded on the throne by his 13-year-old son. At the time, Zhao Zheng was still young, so Lü Buwei acted as the regent prime minister of the S
2 CONC tate of Qin, which was still waging war against the other six states.
2 CONT
2 CONT Qin Shi Huang had about 50 children, sons about 30, daughters about 15, but most of their names are unknown. He had numerous concubines but never seemed to name an empress
2 CONT
2 CONT Mausoleum
2 CONT The Chinese historian Sima Qian, writing a century after the First Emperor's death, wrote that it took 700,000 men to construct the emperor's mausoleum. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The British historian John Man points out that this figure is larger than any city of the world at that time and calculates that the foundations could have been built by 16,000 men in two years.
2 CONT
2 CONT While Sima Qian never mentioned the terracotta army, the statues were discovered by a group of farmers digging wells on March 29, 1974.  
2 CONT The soldiers were created with a series of mix-and-match clay molds and then further individualized by the artists' hand. Han Purple was also used on some of the warriors. 
2 CONT
2 CONT There are around 6,000 Terracotta Warriors and their purpose was to protect the Emperor in the afterlife from evil spirits. Also among the army are chariots and 40,000 real bronze weapons.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Tomb
2 CONT One of the first projects the young king accomplished while he was alive was the construction of his own tomb. In 215 BC Qin Shi Huang ordered General Meng Tian with 300,000 men to begin construction. Other sources suggested he or
2 CONC dered 720,000 unpaid laborers to build his tomb to specification. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Again, given John Man's observation regarding populations of the time (see paragraph above), these historical estimates are debatable. The main tomb (located at 34°22′52.75″N 109°15′13.06″E) containing the emperor has yet to be op
2 CONC ened and there is evidence suggesting that it remains relatively intact. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sima Qian's description of the tomb includes replicas of palaces and scenic towers, "rare utensils and wonderful objects", 100 rivers made with mercury, representations of "the heavenly bodies", and crossbows rigged to shoot anyon
2 CONC e who tried to break in. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The tomb was built at the foot of Li Mountain, and is only 30 kilometers away from Xi'an. Modern archaeologists have located the tomb, and have inserted probes deep into it. The probes revealed abnormally high quantities of mercur
2 CONC y, some 100 times the naturally occurring rate, suggesting that some parts of the legend are credible. Secrets were maintained, as most of the workmen who built the tomb were killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qin_Shi_Huang
