1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Liu An''' 劉安 (?179–122 b.c.e.), King of Huainan 淮南, Western Han writer, thinker, and ruler.
2 CONT
2 CONT Liu An was the son of Liu Chang 劉長, King of Huainan from 196 b.c.e.to 174 b.c.e. Liu Chang staged an abortive coup in 174 BCE and died on the way to exile in Shu. The kingship in Huainan remained vacant until 164 BCE, when Emperor
2 CONC  Wen (r. 180–157 b.c.e.) named Liu An King of Huainan. In 154 BCE, Liu Pi 劉濞 King of Wu (r. 195–154 BCE) tried to induce Liu An to support his insurrection, but Liu An sided with the emperor. Liu An was fond of literature and enjo
2 CONC yed playing the zither. He recruited to his capital at Shouchun 壽春 (modern Shou 壽 county, Anhui) a large group of “guests” who were skilled at writing ''fu''. Some of the guests helped him compile the ''Huainanzi'' 淮南子 that is arg
2 CONC uably the most important philosophical work of the early Former Han. Liu An presented this book to Emperor Wu (r. 141–87 BCE) when he visited Chang’an in 139 BCE. Because he was Liu An’s nephew, Emperor Wu, who had just ascended t
2 CONC he imperial throne in 141 BCE, was quite deferential to Liu An. Whenever he had any communication sent to Huainan, he always had Sima Xiangru 司馬相如 (179–117 b.c.e.) and other skilled writers polish the prose. In the 120’s, Liu An b
2 CONC egan to harbor ambitions for the imperial throne. When the imperial authorities came to arrest him 122, he committed suicide.
2 CONT
2 CONT The biography of Liu An in the ''Han shu'' says that Liu An invited several thousand scholars to his court. Although this number may be an exaggeration, there is no question that Liu An’s court included a large number of learned m
2 CONC en. According to the biography, Liu An and his court scholars wrote the ''Nei shu'' 內書 (Internal writings) in twenty-one ''pian'', the ''Wai shu'' 外書 (External writings) in thirty-three ''pian'', and the ''Zhong pian'' 中篇 in eight
2 CONC  ''pian''. The ''Nei shu'' is the same work that is better known as the ''Huainanzi''. The ''Wai shu'' is not longer extant, but according to the Tang scholar Yan Shigu 顏師古 (581–645) it consists of “diverse unclassified theories.”
2 CONC  The ''Zhong pian'' was a work on alchemy. When Liu An visited Emperor Wu in Chang’an in 139 b.c.e. he presented him with a copy of the ''Nei shu''. Emperor Wu was so impressed with it, he had it secreted away in the imperial arch
2 CONC ives.
2 CONT
2 CONT The ''Huainanzi'', also known as ''Huainan honglie'' 淮南鴻烈 (Grand achievements of [the King of] Huainan), is a work in twenty-one chapters. Its textual history is complicated. For a detailed textual history see the study by Harold 
2 CONC Roth listed below. Already in the Han period it had received commentaries by Ma Rong 馬融 (79–166), Lu Zhi 盧植 (d. 192), Gao You 高誘 (ca. 160–220) and Xu Shen 許慎 (ca. 48–ca. 147). The work is a multi-authored compilation. How much Liu
2 CONC  An actually contributed to it is impossible to determine. The ''Huainanzi'' is encyclopedic in scope and attempts to present an account of all known knowledge.
2 CONT
2 CONT Liu An was also a prolific ''fu'' writer. The monograph on bibliography of the ''Han shu'' records eighty-two ''fu'' compositions under his name. It also lists fourteen pieces by his courtiers. The titles of these pieces is not kn
2 CONC own. Only two poems attributed to Liu An survive, the “Zhao yin shi” 招隱士 (Summon the recluse) and “Pingfeng fu” 屛風賦 (''Fu'' on the screen), the authenticity of which is not certain. Liu An also wrote a commentary to the “Li sao” t
2 CONC hat he presented to Emperor Wu.
2 CONT
2 CONT David R. Knechtges
2 CONT
2 CONT Citation
2 CONT * 劉安 Liu An, Brill’s Chinese Reference Shelf. Brill Online, 2017. http://chinesereferenceshelf.brillonline.com/ancient-literature/entries/SIM-300228
